{
  "result": "I have always loved the hour just before the library closes for the night. The light turns amber through the tall windows, and dust drifts slowly above the tables like tiny, patient stars. People speak more softly then, as if they already belong partly to their dreams. I like to wander between the shelves without looking for anything in particular. A spine catches my eye, a title I have never heard, and suddenly an afternoon opens that I had not planned. That is the quiet magic of free attention: the world is not demanding an answer, only offering companionship. Outside, buses sigh at the curb and bicycles tick past in the cooling air. Inside, a child turns pages too fast to read them, loving the pictures, the weight, the secret rustle. An old man copies a poem into a notebook with a pencil worn down to a stub. Nobody here is impressive, and nobody needs to be. We are just borrowing warmth from one another and from books that will outlive our marginal notes. When the librarian finally flicks the lights, we gather our coats with reluctant gratitude. The street feels larger after reading, not smaller, because every passing face now suggests an unwritten chapter. I walk home slowly, carrying nothing I checked out, yet somehow carrying everything I needed for tomorrow. Perhaps freedom is exactly this: time unmeasured, curiosity unhurried, and a mind allowed to wander until it returns, quietly changed, to the ordinary beautiful business of living again.",
